WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.794
A payment service sends a settlement file to its bank, 70 milliseconds away there and back.

2
00:00:09.794 --> 00:00:13.380
TCP sends it in segments of about 1,500 bytes.

3
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:18.416
The simplest rule: send one segment, then wait for the bank's reply, an ACK.

4
00:00:18.416 --> 00:00:22.720
That's about 20 kilobytes a second, and the link sits almost empty.

5
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.823
So TCP keeps many segments in flight, sent but not yet acknowledged.

6
00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:29.488
How many is the window.

7
00:00:29.488 --> 00:00:32.456
A window of 10 sends 10 every round trip.

8
00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:36.017
Speed is the window divided by the round trip.

9
00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:40.945
Ten segments every 70 milliseconds is about 200 kilobytes a second.

10
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:43.783
Bigger is faster, until the path is full.

11
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:49.856
This one holds 100 segments, counting a router's queue, and the router drops what doesn't fit.

12
00:00:49.856 --> 00:00:52.183
Nobody tells the sender that number.

13
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:53.310
So it probes.

14
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:58.171
It starts at 10, and every ACK adds one more: 20, then 40, then 80.

15
00:00:58.171 --> 00:01:01.581
Doubling every round trip is called slow start.

16
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:03.694
Round five sends 160.

17
00:01:03.694 --> 00:01:07.948
Only 100 fit, and the router drops the other 60.

18
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.529
The bank keeps repeating its last ACK.

19
00:01:10.529 --> 00:01:14.260
Three duplicate ACKs tell the sender one segment is missing.

20
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.933
It resends it and halves the window, to 80.

21
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:20.230
Now it adds just one segment per round trip.

22
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:27.621
Twenty round trips later it's back at 100, and at 101 the queue overflows again: the window halves to 50.

23
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.161
Then up by one, and down by half, over and over: a sawtooth.

24
00:01:32.161 --> 00:01:33.106
This is Reno.

25
00:01:33.106 --> 00:01:37.612
Linux's default, CUBIC, cuts less, but also backs off on loss.

26
00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:43.344
On average it fills three quarters of the path: about 1.6 megabytes a second.

27
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.482
Each loss halves the speed too.

28
00:01:45.482 --> 00:01:50.364
That's why losing one packet in a hundred can make a download ten times slower.

29
00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:52.661
The sender can't see the network.

30
00:01:52.661 --> 00:01:59.263
It grows the window until a loss, then halves it, and its speed is the window divided by the round trip.
